# A ward that cannot be covered, and why.
#
# This model exists to be infeasible. Every other example here is a plan; this
# one is the question a planner actually asks when there is no plan — which
# rule is in the way, by how much, and what would have to change.
#
# The arithmetic is small enough to check by eye. Two nights each need two
# nurses, so four nurse-nights are required. Cleo is on leave all week, and
# nobody may work more than one night, so ada and bram can supply two between
# them. Four needed, two available.
#
# Asked to solve this, a solver says "infeasible" and stops. Asked to diagnose
# it, the platform relaxes every rule at once, finds the fewest that have to
# give and the least they can give by, and reports those with the statements
# and the parameters already attached to them. Nothing in this file is relaxed
# to do that: the elastic copy lives for the length of the diagnosis.
#
# The two scenarios are the two things a planner does next. One buys a little
# help and the shortfall drops from two to one — still no plan, but a measured
# distance from one. The other buys enough, and there is nothing to diagnose.

problem ward_cover "Ward night cover"
  "Staff every night with the nurses it needs, from those who can work and "
  "within the nights each of them may be asked for."

set Nurses = ada, bram, cleo of nurse
set Nights = mon, tue of night
  labels { mon: "Monday night", tue: "Tuesday night" }

param needed[Nights] "Nurses this night needs" = { mon: 2, tue: 2 }

param can_work[Nurses, Nights] default 1 "1 when the nurse can work that night" = {
    (cleo, mon): 0, (cleo, tue): 0 }

param nights_allowed[Nurses] default 1 "Most nights any one nurse may be asked for"

var on_duty[Nurses, Nights] binary means "Put this nurse on this night"

constraint cover_the_night "Each night is staffed to the level it needs"
  category regulatory
  because "Night cover is a licensing condition, not a preference, which is why "
          "it is stated as a rule that holds rather than one with a price"
  forall n in Nights:
    sum(on_duty[p, n] for p in Nurses) >= needed[n]

constraint only_if_they_can_work "Nobody is rostered on a night they cannot work"
  category physical
  forall p in Nurses, n in Nights where can_work[p, n] == 0:
    on_duty[p, n] == 0

constraint within_nights_allowed "Nobody is asked for more nights than allowed"
  category regulatory
  forall p in Nurses:
    sum(on_duty[p, n] for n in Nights) <= nights_allowed[p]

minimize nights_worked "Nights worked in total" unit "nurse-nights":
  sum(on_duty[p, n] for p in Nurses, n in Nights)

assume leave_is_absolute "A nurse on leave cannot be rostered at any price"
  because "It is the one rule here that no amount of need can buy, which is what "
          "separates it from the two that could in principle be argued"
  affects only_if_they_can_work

scenario agency_for_one_night "An agency nurse is engaged for Monday"
  "One extra pair of hands, on one night, within the usual limit. Monday can "
  "now be staffed, which leaves Tuesday one nurse short instead of two: the "
  "ward is still impossible, and the diagnosis says by how much less."
  set can_work[cleo, mon] to 1

scenario agency_for_the_week "An agency nurse is engaged for both nights"
  "The same nurse, released from the one-night limit. Four nurse-nights are "
  "now available for four needed, and there is a plan — so there is nothing "
  "to diagnose."
  set can_work[cleo, mon] to 1
  set can_work[cleo, tue] to 1
  set nights_allowed[cleo] to 2
